  "summary": "A traditional farmhouse construction method using turf or sod as roofing material with stone or timber walls.",
  "description": "Features thick turf layers providing insulation and blending with the landscape; developed in response to scarce timber resources and harsh northern climates. The turf gradually grows vegetation, creating a living roof that provides excellent insulation.",
  "culturalContext": "Turf-roofed farmhouses — Iceland's torfbær (UNESCO-listed Glaumbær, Laufás), Norwegian grass roofs, Faroese ì Føroyum settlements — built with what the North Atlantic offered: stone walls, birch frames, and turf layers providing insulation (R-value) against brutal winters. The soft green-roof silhouette — windows peeking under grass banks — became national-icon material (Icelandic turf houses on stamps, Norway's reconstructed villages) and a modern green-roof movement's ancestral precedent.",
